The direction <vectors> are $v=(-1,-1,1)$ and $w=(1,1,2)$. Their <scalar product> is $v\cdot w=-1-1+2=0$. Both are nonzero, so the lines are perpendicular:
$$
\boxed{\theta=\frac\pi2=90^\circ}.
$$

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Equating Cartesian components gives $1-s=t$, $-s=-1+t$, and $2+s=2t$. Substitution of $t=1-s$ into the last equation gives $s=0$, hence $t=1$. Both parametrizations therefore give the intersection point
$$
\boxed{\mathbf r=(1,0,2)}.
$$

