= Solution
{parent=a}

The <characteristic polynomial> is
$$
\det\begin{pmatrix}-\lambda&a\\a&-\lambda\end{pmatrix}=\lambda^2-a^2.
$$
Its roots give the <eigenvalues>
$$
\boxed{\lambda=a,\quad\lambda=-a}.
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $\lambda=a$, the components of an <eigenvector> are equal; for $\lambda=-a$, they are opposite. Dividing each <vector> by its <Euclidean norm> gives
$$
\boxed{u_+=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}\quad(\lambda=a),\qquad
u_-=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}\quad(\lambda=-a)}.
$$
They are mutually <orthogonal> and have unit norm. The labels specify their <eigenvalues> even when $a<0$.

= 4
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=4}

Use the <Taylor series> $\cos x=1-x^2/2+O(x^4)$. Squaring gives $\cos^2x=1-x^2+O(x^4)$, and hence
$$
\boxed{x^3\cos^2x=x^3-x^5+O(x^7)}.
$$
The first two nonzero terms are therefore $x^3$ and $-x^5$.

= 5
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=5}

Apply the <cosine> double-angle identity twice:
$$
\cos^4x=\left(\frac{1+\cos2x}{2}\right)^2
=\frac38+\frac12\cos2x+\frac18\cos4x.
$$
This finite trigonometric sum is already the <Fourier series>. In increasing harmonic order, including the constant term, the first two nonzero terms are
$$
\boxed{\frac38+\frac12\cos2x}.
$$

