= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For real coordinates, work on $y>0$. The <chain rule> gives $\xi_y=y^{-1/2}$ and $\xi_{yy}=-\tfrac12y^{-3/2}$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial^2u}{\partial y^2}
=u''(\xi)(\xi_y)^2+u'(\xi)\xi_{yy}
=\frac{u''(\xi)}y-\frac{u'(\xi)}{2y^{3/2}}}.
$$
The <derivatives> on the right are with respect to $\xi$. Behaviour at $y=0$ requires separate regularity of the composed <function>.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The same <chain rule> gives $u_{xx}=u''(\xi)$ and $u_y=u'(\xi)/\sqrt y$. Substituting these and part (a) into the <partial differential equation> gives
$$
u_{xx}-\frac12u_y-yu_{yy}
=u''-\frac{u'}{2\sqrt y}-y\left(\frac{u''}y-\frac{u'}{2y^{3/2}}\right)=\boxed{0}.
$$
Thus every twice-differentiable profile $u(\xi)$ gives a solution on $y>0$.

= 10
