Parametrize the path by $\mathbf r(t)=(x(t),y(t),z(t))$, from $t=a$ to $t=b$. For the <conservative vector field> $\mathbf F=\nabla\Phi$, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\mathbf F\cdot\frac{d\mathbf r}{dt}
=\Phi_x\frac{dx}{dt}+\Phi_y\frac{dy}{dt}+\Phi_z\frac{dz}{dt}
=\frac{d}{dt}\Phi(\mathbf r(t)).
$$
Integrating proves the <fundamental theorem for line integrals>:
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=\Phi(\mathbf r(b))-\Phi(\mathbf r(a))}.
$$
Only the endpoints matter, so the integral is independent of the connecting path.

= b
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a twice continuously <differentiable> scalar potential, the <curl> of its <gradient> is
$$
\nabla\times\nabla\Phi
=(\Phi_{zy}-\Phi_{yz},\ \Phi_{xz}-\Phi_{zx},\ \Phi_{yx}-\Phi_{xy}).
$$
Equality of mixed <partial derivatives> makes every component zero. Thus
$$
\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F=0}.
$$
This is the <curl of a gradient> identity.

= c
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Differentiate the components directly:
$$
\begin{aligned}
(\nabla\times\mathbf F)_x&=\partial_y(-3xz^2-1)-\partial_z(x^2-2y)=0,\\
(\nabla\times\mathbf F)_y&=\partial_z(2xy-z^3)-\partial_x(-3xz^2-1)=-3z^2+3z^2=0,\\
(\nabla\times\mathbf F)_z&=\partial_x(x^2-2y)-\partial_y(2xy-z^3)=2x-2x=0.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus $\boxed{\nabla\times\mathbf F=0}$. The field is smooth on all of $\mathbb R^3$, a <simply connected domain>, so the zero-curl condition is sufficient for a <conservative vector field>. The domain condition matters: zero <curl> alone need not imply a global potential on a region with holes.

= d
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Evaluate the <line integral> along the three coordinate segments, treating coordinates not changing along a segment as constants. On the first segment, $y=z=0$, so $F_x=0$ and the contribution is zero. On the second segment,
$$
\int_0^yF_y(x,\eta,0)\,d\eta=\int_0^y(x^2-2\eta)\,d\eta=x^2y-y^2.
$$
On the final segment,
$$
\int_0^zF_z(x,y,\zeta)\,d\zeta
=\int_0^z(-3x\zeta^2-1)\,d\zeta=-xz^3-z.
$$
The <fundamental theorem for line integrals> therefore gives
$$
\boxed{\Phi(x,y,z)=x^2y-y^2-xz^3-z+C}.
$$
Taking its <gradient> verifies each component of the field. The constant is $C=\Phi(0,0,0)$; the field specifies only potential differences because the <gradient> of every constant is zero.

= e
{parent=13}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

For $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t^2)$, the tangent <vector> is $\mathbf r'(t)=(1,1,2t)$. Substitute into the field:
$$
\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))=(2t^2-t^6,\ t^2-2t,\ -3t^5-1).
$$
Its <scalar product> with the tangent is $3t^2-4t-7t^6$. Hence the explicitly parametrized <line integral> is
$$
\boxed{\int_C\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r
=\int_1^2(3t^2-4t-7t^6)\,dt
=[t^3-2t^2-t^7]_1^2=-126}.
$$
As a check, the potential from part (d) has values $-128+C$ at $(2,2,4)$ and $-2+C$ at $(1,1,1)$, giving the same difference.

