= Solution
{parent=i}

Normalization of the <probability mass function> requires
$$
1=A\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{\lambda^k}{k!}=Ae^\lambda.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{A=e^{-\lambda}}.
$$
The resulting law is the <Poisson distribution> with parameter $\lambda$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a nonnegative integer $K$, add the probabilities of the disjoint events with $0,1,\ldots,K$ goals. The <cumulative distribution function> is
$$
\boxed{P(X\leq K)=e^{-\lambda}\sum_{k=0}^{K}\frac{\lambda^k}{k!}}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The zero-goal term contributes nothing to the <expected value>. Shift the summation index in the remaining terms:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mu&=e^{-\lambda}\sum_{k=1}^\infty k\frac{\lambda^k}{k!}
=\lambda e^{-\lambda}\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{\lambda^{k-1}}{(k-1)!}\\
&=\lambda e^{-\lambda}e^\lambda=\boxed{\lambda}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The exponential series justifies the convergent reindexing. Thus the parameter is the <mean> goals per game.

= c
{parent=14}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The intended season calculation needs the additional assumption that scores in different games are <independent random variables>. The printed statement gives their common one-game distribution but does not explicitly state <independence>; identical marginals alone do not determine season probabilities.

Under <independence>, each game has <probability> $1-P(X=0)=1-e^{-\lambda}$ of at least one goal, so
$$
\boxed{P(\text{at least one goal in every game})=(1-e^{-\lambda})^{10}}.
$$
Without <independence> this product is not determined by the supplied marginal distribution.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Continue with the independent-games assumption from part (i). Exactly one of the ten games must have one goal, while the other nine have zero. The ten choices of the scoring game are disjoint, so
$$
\boxed{P(\text{one goal in the season})
=10\,(\lambda e^{-\lambda})(e^{-\lambda})^9
=10\lambda e^{-10\lambda}}.
$$

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Under <independence>, there are two disjoint ways to score two goals in total. One game can contain both, with <probability>
$$
10\left(e^{-\lambda}\frac{\lambda^2}{2}\right)(e^{-\lambda})^9
=5\lambda^2e^{-10\lambda}.
$$
Alternatively, two different games can contain one goal each, with <probability>
$$
\binom{10}{2}(\lambda e^{-\lambda})^2(e^{-\lambda})^8
=45\lambda^2e^{-10\lambda}.
$$
Adding gives
$$
\boxed{P(\text{two goals in the season})=50\lambda^2e^{-10\lambda}}.
$$
This also follows from the <sum of independent Poisson random variables>: the season total is Poisson with parameter $10\lambda$, whose <probability> at two is $e^{-10\lambda}(10\lambda)^2/2!$.

