= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The PDF diagram specifies the prism as
$$
0\leq x\leq2,\qquad0\leq y\leq4-2x,\qquad0\leq z\leq1.
$$
Its triangular horizontal cross-section has area four. Calculate the outward <flux> on each face using its outward <normal vector>.

On $x=0$, the normal is $-\mathbf e_x$ and $F_x=0$, so the <flux> is zero. On $y=0$, the normal is $-\mathbf e_y$, giving
$$
\Phi_{y=0}=-\int_0^1\int_0^2(2x-4)^2\,dx\,dz=-\frac{32}{3}.
$$
On the sloping vertical face $y+2x=4$, the outward normal is proportional to $(2,1,0)$. Both $F_x$ and $F_y$ vanish there, while $F_z$ is tangent to the face, so the <flux> is zero.

On the bottom $z=0$, the normal is $-\mathbf e_z$ and $F_z=1$, so the <flux> is minus the base area, namely $-4$. On the top $z=1$, $F_z=0$, so the <flux> is zero. Thus all five face values and their sum are
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\text{face}&x=0&y=0&y+2x=4&z=0&z=1\\\hline
\text{outward flux}&0&-32/3&0&-4&0
\end{array}\qquad\Phi_{\mathrm{total}}=-\frac{44}{3}}.
$$

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Differentiate the corresponding components:
$$
\boxed{\nabla\cdot\mathbf F=0+2(y+2x-4)-2z=2y+4x-8-2z}.
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use the prism bounds read from the PDF diagram and the <divergence> from part (ii):
$$
\begin{aligned}
\int_V\nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV
&=\int_0^2\int_0^{4-2x}\int_0^1(2y+4x-8-2z)\,dz\,dy\,dx\\
&=\int_0^2\int_0^{4-2x}(2y+4x-9)\,dy\,dx.
\end{aligned}
$$
Set $A=4-2x$ in the inner evaluation. Since $4x-9=-2A-1$, the inner integral is $A^2-(2A+1)A=-A^2-A$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\int_V\nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV
=-\int_0^2[(4-2x)^2+(4-2x)]\,dx
=-\frac{32}{3}-4=-\frac{44}{3}}.
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

The two independently computed answers agree:
$$
\boxed{\oint_S\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n\,dS
=\int_V\nabla\cdot\mathbf F\,dV=-\frac{44}{3}}.
$$
This verifies the <divergence theorem>. Its hypotheses hold because the field is smooth and the prism has a closed piecewise smooth boundary with consistently outward normals.

