{parent=a}

First divide each of the first two equations by two. Then
$$
\boxed{K=\begin{pmatrix}3/2&-1/2&0\\-1/2&3/2&0\\0&0&4\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
The equal-component and opposite-component directions diagonalize the upper block, while the third coordinate is independent. Direct multiplication gives the <eigenvalues> and unit <eigenvectors>
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\gamma&\text{normalized eigenvector}\\\hline
1&\frac1{\sqrt2}(1,1,0)^T\\
2&\frac1{\sqrt2}(1,-1,0)^T\\
4&(0,0,1)^T
\end{array}}.
$$
They form an <orthonormal eigenbasis> because $K$ is a real <symmetric matrix>.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Substitute $\mathbf y=\mathbf v e^{-\gamma t}$ into the unforced system. It gives $(K-\gamma I)\mathbf v=0$, so a nonzero single-exponential solution must use one of the eigendirections in part (a):
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
\mathbf y_1(t)&=A(1,1,0)^Te^{-t},\\
\mathbf y_2(t)&=B(1,-1,0)^Te^{-2t},\\
\mathbf y_3(t)&=C(0,0,1)^Te^{-4t}.
\end{aligned}}
$$
These are all nonzero solutions having one common decay constant. Their arbitrary <linear combination> is the general unforced solution; all decay because the three <eigenvalues> are positive.

= c
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At a <steady state>, $K\mathbf y_0=\mathbf f_0$. All <eigenvalues> of $K$ are nonzero, so it is invertible and
$$
\boxed{\mathbf y_0=K^{-1}\mathbf f_0,\qquad
K^{-1}=\frac14\begin{pmatrix}3&1&0\\1&3&0\\0&0&1\end{pmatrix}}.
$$
For the specified constant source this gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbf y_0=(1,1,1/4)^T}.
$$
Indeed the first two rows of $K\mathbf y_0$ each give one, and the third gives $4(1/4)=1$.

= d
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Take the switch-off time to be $t=0$. The state remains <continuous> through the source step, so $\mathbf y(0)=(1,1,1/4)^T$. Its eigendirection decomposition is
$$
\mathbf y(0)=(1,1,0)^T+\frac14(0,0,1)^T.
$$
The antisymmetric mode has zero amplitude because the first two initial components are equal. Applying the decay factors from part (b),
$$
\boxed{\mathbf y(t)=(e^{-t},\ e^{-t},\ \tfrac14e^{-4t})^T\qquad(t\geq0)}.
$$
The first two responses decay at rate one and the third at rate four, approaching the new zero-source <steady state>.

