= a
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For a real $2\pi$-periodic square-integrable <function>, use the <Fourier series> convention
$$
f(x)\sim\frac{a_0}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^\infty[a_n\cos nx+b_n\sin nx],
$$
with <Fourier coefficients>
$$
\boxed{a_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)\cos nx\,dx\quad(n\geq0),\qquad
b_n=\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)\sin nx\,dx\quad(n\geq1)}.
$$
The trigonometric <orthogonality> relations are $\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\cos nx\cos mx\,dx=\pi\delta_{nm}$ for positive $n,m$, with the same formula for sines; sine-cosine products integrate to zero. The constant has squared integral $2\pi$ and is <orthogonal> to every positive harmonic. Thus the squared norm of the $N$th partial sum is
$$
\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f_N(x)^2\,dx
=\frac{a_0^2}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^N(a_n^2+b_n^2).
$$
Taking the limit in the <L2 space> gives the Fourier-series <Parseval identity>:
$$
\boxed{\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(x)^2\,dx
=\frac{a_0^2}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^\infty(a_n^2+b_n^2)}.
$$
Square integrability is the natural hypothesis for this finite norm identity; additional smoothness can be used when pointwise convergence is required.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <function> is odd, so its constant and <cosine> coefficients are zero. Its <sine> coefficient is
$$
b_n=\frac2\pi I_n,\qquad I_n=\int_0^\pi(x^3-\pi^2x)\sin nx\,dx.
$$
The <polynomial> vanishes at both integration endpoints. Two <integrations by parts> therefore give
$$
I_n=\frac1n\int_0^\pi(3x^2-\pi^2)\cos nx\,dx
=-\frac1{n^2}\int_0^\pi6x\sin nx\,dx.
$$
One more integration yields
$$
\int_0^\pi x\sin nx\,dx
=\left[-\frac{x\cos nx}{n}+\frac{\sin nx}{n^2}\right]_0^\pi
=-\frac{\pi(-1)^n}{n}.
$$
Consequently $I_n=6\pi(-1)^n/n^3$ and $b_n=12(-1)^n/n^3$. Hence
$$
\boxed{g(x)=12\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^n}{n^3}\sin nx,\qquad p=3}.
$$
The periodic continuation is <continuous> at $\pm\pi$, where its values are zero, so the series represents it there as well as in the open interval.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For this odd <function>, the <Parseval identity> from part (a) gives
$$
\frac1\pi\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}(x^3-\pi^2x)^2\,dx
=144\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac1{n^6}.
$$
The integrand is even. Expanding and integrating it gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac2\pi\int_0^\pi(x^6-2\pi^2x^4+\pi^4x^2)\,dx
&=2\pi^6\left(\frac17-\frac25+\frac13\right)\\
&=\frac{16\pi^6}{105}.
\end{aligned}
$$
Dividing by $144$ therefore proves
$$
\boxed{\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac1{n^6}=\frac{\pi^6}{945}}.
$$
This evaluates the <Riemann zeta function> at six by using the norm of a simple <polynomial>.

