= Solution
{parent=a}

As in the diagram, an inscribed <cylinder> rests on $z=0$, is coaxial with the paraboloid, and has its top rim on the curved boundary. Thus
$$
h+\frac{Hr^2}{R^2}=H,\qquad V_c=\pi r^2h.
$$
Allowing contact with the boundary is the usual meaning of *inscribed*. Form the <Lagrange multiplier> <function>
$$
\mathcal L=\pi r^2h-\lambda\left(h+\frac{Hr^2}{R^2}-H\right).
$$
At a nondegenerate stationary <cylinder>, its two <derivative> equations are
$$
2\pi rh=\lambda\frac{2Hr}{R^2},\qquad \pi r^2=\lambda.
$$
Eliminating $\lambda$ gives $h=Hr^2/R^2$. The <constraint> then gives $h=H/2$ and $r=R/\sqrt2$, so
$$
\boxed{V_{c,\max}=\frac{\pi R^2H}{4}}.
$$
The degenerate endpoints $r=0$ or $h=0$ have zero <volume>, consistent with the positive stationary value being the <maximum>. If the strict inequalities defining the open interior were interpreted as forbidding boundary contact, the same value would instead be a supremum approached by smaller cylinders.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use the diagram's labels: $a,b$ are horizontal side lengths and $c$ is the height of the <rectangular parallelepiped>. Its four top vertices are at horizontal distance $\sqrt{a^2+b^2}/2$ from the axis. Thus the contact <constraint> and <volume> are
$$
c+\frac{H(a^2+b^2)}{4R^2}=H,\qquad V_p=abc.
$$
Apply a <Lagrange multiplier>:
$$
\mathcal L=abc-\lambda\left(c+\frac{H(a^2+b^2)}{4R^2}-H\right).
$$
For positive dimensions, the <derivative> equations are
$$
bc=\lambda\frac{Ha}{2R^2},\qquad
ac=\lambda\frac{Hb}{2R^2},\qquad ab=\lambda.
$$
Substituting the last equation into the first two gives
$$
c=\frac{Ha^2}{2R^2}=\frac{Hb^2}{2R^2}.
$$
Hence $a=b$, and the <constraint> becomes $2c=H$. Therefore
$$
\boxed{a=b=R,\qquad c=\frac H2,\qquad V_{p,\max}=\frac{R^2H}{2}}.
$$
These are the <maximal cylinder and box in an elliptic paraboloid> dimensions for the rectangular case. A horizontal rotation of the square footprint gives the same <volume> by rotational symmetry.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The ratio of the two maximal volumes is
$$
\boxed{\frac{V_{c,\max}}{V_{p,\max}}=\frac\pi2>1}.
$$
Thus **the <cylinder> has the larger <maximum> <volume>**, by a factor $\pi/2$. Both optimal shapes have height $H/2$, while their horizontal cross-sections have areas $\pi R^2/2$ and $R^2$, respectively.

