= Solution
{parent=i}

For the solution through $x=0$, work on the connected domain $x>-1$. While $y>0$, <separation of variables> gives
$$
\frac{dy}{\sqrt y}=-\frac{dx}{1+x},\qquad 2\sqrt y=-\ln(1+x)+C.
$$
The <initial condition> gives $C=2$, so
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\left(1-\frac12\ln(1+x)\right)^2\quad(-1<x\leq e^2-1)}.
$$
The restriction matters: the unsquared equation requires $1-\frac12\ln(1+x)\geq0$. The solution reaches zero at $x_*=e^2-1$. Continuing the squared formula beyond that point would give a positive <derivative>, contradicting the original negative square-root right-hand side.

The nonnegative forward continuation is instead
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\begin{cases}
\left(1-\frac12\ln(1+x)\right)^2,&-1<x\leq e^2-1,\\
0,&x\geq e^2-1.
\end{cases}}
$$
The two pieces join with $y'=0$, and both satisfy the original equation. Because the right-hand side is nonpositive for $x>-1$, a nonnegative solution cannot leave zero in the forward direction. This is a finite-time extinction phenomenon for a <separable differential equation>.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

This is a <Bernoulli differential equation> with exponent four. On the nonzero branch selected by $y(0)=1$, put $v=y^{-3}$. Multiplying the original equation by $-3y^{-4}$ gives
$$
v'-v=-3e^x.
$$
Its <integrating factor> is $e^{-x}$, so $(e^{-x}v)'=-3$ and $e^{-x}v=C-3x$. The <initial condition> sets $C=1$. Thus
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\frac{e^{-x/3}}{(1-3x)^{1/3}},\qquad -\infty<x<\frac13}.
$$
This is the maximal real solution interval containing zero: the solution diverges as $x\to(1/3)^-$. The real negative branch of the same algebraic formula for $x>1/3$ is disconnected from this initial-value solution. The original equation also admits $y=0$, but it does not satisfy the prescribed <initial condition>.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a nonzero separated solution $u=X(x)T(t)$ of the <reaction-diffusion equation>, substitution and division by $XT$ gives
$$
\frac{T'}T+1=\frac{X''}X.
$$
The two sides depend on different variables, so both equal a separation constant. The homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions> select a negative spatial constant, written $-k^2$:
$$
X''+k^2X=0,\qquad T'+(1+k^2)T=0.
$$
For $k>0$, $X=A\sin kx+B\cos kx$. The boundary value at zero makes $B=0$; the boundary value at $L$ then requires $kL=n\pi$, with $n=1,2,\ldots$. A zero spatial constant gives only a linear <function> vanishing at both endpoints, hence the zero <function>; a positive spatial constant similarly gives no nonzero admissible hyperbolic solution. Therefore
$$
\boxed{X_n(x)=A_n\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L},\qquad
T_n(t)=B_n\exp\left[-\left(1+\frac{n^2\pi^2}{L^2}\right)t\right]}.
$$
By <superposition>, the <general solution> with the stated boundary conditions is
$$
\boxed{u(x,t)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty c_n\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}
\exp\left[-\left(1+\frac{n^2\pi^2}{L^2}\right)t\right]}.
$$
The coefficients are fixed only if initial data are supplied: for $u(x,0)=f(x)$, they are the <Fourier sine series> coefficients $c_n=(2/L)\int_0^L f(x)\sin(n\pi x/L)\,dx$. Here they remain arbitrary subject to convergence. Every mode has the ordinary diffusive decay rate plus the additional reaction decay rate one.
